activation	Compute current body text	2026-10-20T07:39:56.2084084Z text: Black breaks code in multiline assertion\n\n\n`pip install -U black`\n2. Use the online formatter at (black.vercel.app/redacted), which will use\n   the latest main branch.\n3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n   - clone this repository;\n   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n   - run `pip install --group tests`\n   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest -n auto`; and\n   - run `black` like you did last time.\n-->\n\n**Describe the bug**\n\nRunning Black on the example code below introduces a change in the meaning of the code.\n\n**To Reproduce**\n\nFor example, take this code:\n\n```python\nassert (\n    # my comment\n    my_var == 999 if abc == "TEST" else 0\n)\n```\n\nBlack updates it to:\n\n```python\nassert (\n    # my comment\n    my_var == 999\n    if abc == "TEST"\n    else 0\n)\n```\n\nThis introduces a bug:\n- Original code: when `abc == "TEST"` this results in `assert my_var == 0`\n- Updated code: when `abc == "TEST"` this results in `assert 0`\n\n**Expected behavior**\n\nAdding parentheses would fix it. For example:\n\n```python\nassert (\n    # my comment\n    my_var == (\n        999 if abc == "TEST" else 0\n    )\n)\n```\n\n**Environment**\n\n- Black's version: 25.11.0\n- OS and Python version: MacOS, Python 3.9.6\n\n**Additional context**
activation	Compute current body text	2026-10-20T07:39:56.2100574Z title: Black breaks code in multiline assertion
activation	Compute current body text	2026-10-20T07:39:56.2114954Z body: `pip install -U black`\n2. Use the online formatter at (black.vercel.app/redacted), which will use\n   the latest main branch.\n3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n   - clone this repository;\n   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n   - run `pip install --group tests`\n   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest -n auto`; and\n   - run `black` like you did last time.\n-->\n\n**Describe the bug**\n\nRunning Black on the example code below introduces a change in the meaning of the code.\n\n**To Reproduce**\n\nFor example, take this code:\n\n```python\nassert (\n    # my comment\n    my_var == 999 if abc == "TEST" else 0\n)\n```\n\nBlack updates it to:\n\n```python\nassert (\n    # my comment\n    my_var == 999\n    if abc == "TEST"\n    else 0\n)\n```\n\nThis introduces a bug:\n- Original code: when `abc == "TEST"` this results in `assert my_var == 0`\n- Updated code: when `abc == "TEST"` this results in `assert 0`\n\n**Expected behavior**\n\nAdding parentheses would fix it. For example:\n\n```python\nassert (\n    # my comment\n    my_var == (\n        999 if abc == "TEST" else 0\n    )\n)\n```\n\n**Environment**\n\n- Black's version: 25.11.0\n- OS and Python version: MacOS, Python 3.9.6\n\n**Additional context**
